The task requires finding the minimal rank tensor decomposition for $5 \times 5$ matrix multiplication. The current program provides the schoolbook decomposition with rank $5 \times 5 \times 5 = 125$.

A significant improvement can be achieved by using the **Strassen algorithm** recursively. The Strassen algorithm multiplies $2 \times 2$ matrices using 7 scalar multiplications instead of 8. By extending this to $4 \times 4$ matrices (using $4 \times 4$ Strassen blocks composed of $2 \times 2$ Strassen blocks), we can achieve a rank of $7^2 = 49$ for $4 \times 4$ multiplication.

For $5 \times 5$ multiplication, we can embed the $4 \times 4$ Strassen decomposition into the $5 \times 5$ problem. We treat the $5 \times 5$ matrices as:
$$
\begin{pmatrix} A_{4 \times 4} & a_{4 \times 1} \\ a_{1 \times 4} & a_{1 \times 1} \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} B_{4 \times 4} & b_{4 \times 1} \\ b_{1 \times 4} & b_{1 \times 1} \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix} C_{4 \times 4} & c_{4 \times 1} \\ c_{1 \times 4} & c_{1 \times 1} \end{pmatrix}
$$
The $C_{4 \times 4}$ part can be computed with rank 49. The remaining parts ($c_{4 \times 1}$, $c_{1 \times 4}$, $c_{1 \times 1}$) involve multiplications by vectors or scalars, which can be done with rank equal to the number of such elements (since each output element is a linear combination of input products, but here we can just use the standard rank-1 decompositions for the remaining $5^2 - 4^2 = 9$ output slots? No, the tensor structure is specific).…
